                        =================
                        Installing euclid
                        =================

### COMPILATION AND BASIC USAGE ###

0. You first need to install the pari C library. 
   See <http://pari.math.u-bordeaux.fr/>.
   euclid requires pari version 2.4.2 or later.
   You also need gcc to compile the files.

1. In the directory of the euclid archive, type

      tar xvf euclid-1.0.tar.xz
      cd euclid-1.0
      make

2. You can now run ./euclid to obtain the Euclidean minimum of a
   polynomial. You can also type 
   	       ./euclid "x^2-61" or ./euclid [1,0,-61]
   directly.

### TWEAKING ###

   You can change the parameters of execution by changing the 
   configuration file euclid.cfg. No compilation is required to take
   into account these changes.

### OPTIONS ###

1. You can use OPENMP by putting OMP=yes in the Makefile. You need
   to compile again (make clean && make should do the trick). This may
   actually slow the algorithm in fast cases but it will definitely speed
   it up for slower cases (provided your processor has more than one core).

2. You can use a database of Euclidean minima: 
   a. download the sqlite amalgmation file at 
      <http://www.sqlite.org/download.html>.

   b. extract sqlite3.c and sqlite3.h into the euclid-1.0/src/ directory.

   c. put SQLITE=yes in the Makefile.

   d. (optional) download table_euc.db at
      <http://math.u-bordeaux1.fr/~lezowski/euclid/>, put it in the /db
      directory. If you do not download it, an empty database will be created.
   
   e. compile again (make clean && make).

   Now the program will check if the number field used is in the database 
   (and can add it to the database if it does not exist).
   By default, sqlite3 requires lpthread to make it thread safe, but you can 
   bypass this requirement if you really need to by replacing the line 17 of 
   the Makefile by CFLAGS+= -DSQLITE_THREADSAFE=0 .
